<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Wettin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Wettin was a German dynasty that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Two of its members, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a>, were elected kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> in the eighteenth century. Their reigns, during which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> exercised strong influence over the Commonwealth, are associated with its decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin.jpg" alt="House of Wettin" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wereszczyński · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COA_Wettin.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The oldest member of the family known for certain is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i">Theodoric I</a> of Wettin, who died about 982 and was most probably based in the Liesgau, at the western edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>. Around 1000 the family acquired Wettin Castle on the Saale River, originally built by local Slavic tribes, and took its name from it. Around 1030 the Wettins received the Eastern March as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>. Their prominence there led Emperor Henry IV to invest them with the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> in 1089.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The family advanced steadily within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In 1263 it inherited the landgraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, and in 1350 it gained the Palatinate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. In 1423 it was invested with the Duchy of Saxony, centred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a>, which brought the dignity of prince-elector. In 1485 the sons of Frederick II, Elector of Saxony, divided the lands that had been ruled jointly. The elder son Ernest took the electorate and Thuringia, and his younger brother Albert took the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>, which he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a>.</p><p>The Ernestine branch played an important part in the beginnings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a>. Frederick III founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-wittenberg">University of Wittenberg</a> in 1502 and appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> there in 1512. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> of 1546 and 1547 the Albertine Moritz sided with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, who had promised him the electoral rights. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a>, Johann Friedrich had to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> and the electorate to his cousin, and the Ernestines were left with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, which they divided into many small duchies.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertine-line">Albertines</a> kept most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> together, ruling as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> from 1547 to 1806 and as kings from 1806 to 1918, and two of them were kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> between 1697 and 1763. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> they lost about 40 per cent of their lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815, and Frederick Augustus III lost his throne in 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ernestine courts left a mark on culture. In the 18th century Karl August, Duke of Saxe-Weimar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>, brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-wolfgang-von-goethe">Johann Wolfgang von Goethe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, where what became known as Weimar Classicism took shape. In the 19th century the Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> line gained thrones through marriage, and it supplied kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> from 1831 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from 1908 to 1946, as well as consorts to the rulers of Portugal and the United Kingdom. In 1917, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, the British royal family legally took the name Windsor, after advisers rejected Wettin as unsuitably comic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Wettin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name Saxony itself travelled: carried by the electoral title of Saxe-Wittenberg to the <b>Wettin</b> lands on the upper Elbe, it now names a German state far from the original duchy, whose heartland became Lower Saxony and Westphalia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>When the Ascanian electoral line died out with Albert III in 1422, Emperor Sigismund granted the electorate in 1423 to Frederick the Warlike, Margrave of Meissen of the <b>House of Wettin</b>, as a reward for his support against the Hussites.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A treaty of 1554 settled inheritance on the <b>Wettins</b>, and when George Ernest died without heirs in 1583, the county passed to the Ernestine and Albertine branches of Saxony, with parts to Hesse.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-henneberg">County of Henneberg</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts were vassals of greater neighbours, including the bishops of Halberstadt and the <b>Wettins</b>, and repeatedly pledged or sold lands to pay debts.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-hohnstein">County of Hohnstein</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts were among the leading Thuringian nobles alongside the <b>Wettins</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-schwarzburg">County of Schwarzburg</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Ernestine Wettins</b>, who lost the electorate in 1547, kept lands in Thuringia that were repeatedly divided.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-saxe-weimar">Duchy of Saxe-Weimar</a></li></ul></section>
